          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "837beecf-4239-4b3d-b683-1d611af3da2e",
      "key": "de4c15df-2207-466d-9f25-1e2034a90a43",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Itinerary"
        },
        {
          "alias": "richTextContent",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Ch3\u003EDay 1\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe depart by private executive coach from our designated pick up points. En route to our hotel we visit the National Trust\u2019s Belton House near Grantham. A crowning achievement of Restoration country house architecture, Belton contains important collections of paintings, furniture, tapestries and silver. We continue to the centrally located 3* The Lincoln Hotel for a four night stay on a half board basis.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 2\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe National Trust\u2019s Gunby Hall and Gardens is our destination this morning, a beautiful 18th century house with Victorian gardens. Dating back to 1700, Gunby Hall has links with Tennyson, Darwin and Vaughan Williams. During our visit we explore three floors of the hall, full of interesting collection pieces amassed over generations from 1700 to 1963. We then have the opportunity to explore the eight acre gardens which offer sweeping formal lawns, flower borders, vegetable gardens and wildflower corners. This afternoon we enjoy a guided tour at the International Bomber Command Centre, created to provide a world-class facility to serve as a point for recognition, remembrance and reconciliation for Bomber Command. The centre includes a 31-metre tall spire and memorial walls that feature the names of all those who lost their lives serving or supporting Bomber Command during the Second World War.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 3\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOur guided walking tour of Lincoln commences with a guided tour at the Stonebow, an early 16th century building which forms an archway over the main High Street and is home to Lincoln City Council meetings and the heritage of Lincoln\u0026#39;s local politics. Very much a hidden gem of Lincoln, Stonebow houses numerous pieces of regalia, including the sword of Richard II, Mayor\u2019s Chains of Office and Mayor\u2019s posy ring. We then enjoy a guided tour at Lincoln Cathedral, a fine Gothic building dating back to 1088 and continued in several phases throughout the medieval period. Reputedly the tallest building in the world for 238 years, the eminent Victorian writer John Ruskin declared \u2018I have always held... that the cathedral of Lincoln is out and out the most precious piece of architecture in the British Isles and roughly speaking worth any two other cathedrals we have\u2019. We then spend some time at leisure for lunch in Lincoln. This afternoon we visit Lincoln Castle. Built by William the Conqueror in 1068 on a site occupied since Roman times, Lincoln Castle has dominated the Lincoln skyline for almost one thousand years. The Castle is home to the Lincoln\u0026#39;s own Magna Carta, one of only four original copies dating back to 1215, as well as the 1217 Charter of the Forest. Lincoln Castle is the only place where you can see the two documents together in the world, a unique opportunity to see the documents that shaped society as we know it today.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 4\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWe visit the National Trust\u2019s Workhouse at Southwell this morning, surviving from hundreds that once covered the country and is the least altered today. The austere building, the most complete workhouse in existence, was built in 1824 as a place of last resort for the destitute. Its architecture was influenced by prison design and its harsh regime became a blueprint for workhouses throughout the country. The stories of people who lived and worked here over the years help tell the history of the building\u2019s evolution and prompt reflection on how society has tackled social welfare through time. We continue to the National Trust\u2019s Woolsthorpe Manor, a modest 17th century Lincolnshire manor house and the birthplace and family home of Isaac Newton.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch3\u003EDay 5\u003C/h3\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAfter checking out of our hotel we spend some time at leisure in Lincoln this morning. We then enjoy a guided tour at Doddington Hall, intorducing us to the much loved family home that has never been sold since it was built in 1595 by Robert Smythson, one of England\u2019s foremost Elizabethan architects. The mellow brick exterior with its walled courtyards has barely changed while the interior was redecorated in Georgian times in a light and elegant style. Taking carefully selected comfort stops en route, we return back to our original pick up points.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureDescription",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": ""
        },
        {
          "alias": "featurePropertyFeatureSummary",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": {
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": [],
              "layout": {}
            },
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003ENB:- Please note we reserve the right to alter the above itinerary if required for operational\nreasons. Given the historic nature of the places visited, a certain degree of walking will be involved, and this holiday may not be suitable for those with mobility problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
